# Captain System — Deploy & Tower Script Discipline

These rules apply to **every** code/doc change in this repository. They exist
because Captain System runs on two physical towers (Tower A and Tower B) that
each pull from a remote and rebuild Docker; an unverified push or a malformed
script can take a tower offline minutes before market open.

---

## 1. Dual-remote push is mandatory

Every commit that lands on `main` MUST be pushed to **both** remotes:

- `origin` → `git@github.com:nomaan02/captain-system.git`
- `multi-user` → `git@github.com:nomaan02/captain-multi-user.git`

```bash
git push origin HEAD
git push multi-user HEAD
```

The push is not "done" until both succeed. If `multi-user` rejects with
`non-fast-forward`, fetch and reconcile — never `--force` either remote unless
the user explicitly authorises it.

After pushing, immediately verify both remote heads match the local HEAD:

```bash
git fetch origin && git fetch multi-user
test (git rev-parse HEAD) = (git rev-parse origin/main); and \
test (git rev-parse HEAD) = (git rev-parse multi-user/main); \
    and echo "OK: both remotes synced" or echo "MISMATCH"
```

This is non-negotiable. A tower pulling only one remote that lags behind the
other will run inconsistent code at session open.

---

## 2. Tower scripts are fish-shell, Linux, idempotent, and dependency-safe

Any script the agent gives the user to run on Tower A or Tower B MUST satisfy
all of:

1. **Fish-shell compatible.** No bash-isms (`[[`, `$(...)` for command
   substitution → use `(...)`, no `&&` chains in fish — use `; and`/`; or`,
   no `function name() { … }` — use `function name; …; end`).
2. **Linux-native paths.** No Windows / WSL host mounts, no `/c/Users/...`,
   no `\` separators.
3. **Idempotent.** Running it twice in a row must not break state. Use
   `mkdir -p`, `git pull --ff-only`, `apt install -y`, `type -q name; or …`,
   `set -gx VAR (read existing; or default)`.
4. **Dependency preamble at the top.** Every script begins with a guarded
   prerequisite block that is safe to run on a fresh tower:

   ```fish
   # Helpers (idempotent)
   type -q dco; or function dco
       docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.local.yml $argv
   end
   type -q cap-run; or function cap-run
       set -l script $argv[1]
       set -l rest $argv[2..-1]
       docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.local.yml \
           exec -T -e PYTHONPATH=/app captain-offline \
           python /captain/scripts/$script $rest
   end
   type -q online-run; or function online-run
       set -l script $argv[1]
       set -l rest $argv[2..-1]
       docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.local.yml \
           exec -T -e PYTHONPATH=/app captain-online \
           python3 /app/$script $rest
   end
   type -q cmd-run; or function cmd-run
       set -l script $argv[1]
       set -l rest $argv[2..-1]
       docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.local.yml \
           exec -T -e PYTHONPATH=/app captain-command \
           python3 /app/$script $rest
   end

   # Tower-side packages (apt or snap)
   command -v jq        > /dev/null 2>&1; or sudo apt install -y jq
   command -v redis-cli > /dev/null 2>&1; or sudo apt install -y redis-tools
   ```

5. **Use the helper functions.** `cap-run`, `online-run`, `cmd-run`, and
   `dco` exist precisely so the user does not have to retype the docker
   compose flags. Inline `docker compose -f ... -f ... exec -T …` is forbidden
   in tower instructions; emit `cap-run scriptname.py` instead.
6. **POSIX greps, not ripgrep.** Towers may not have `rg` installed. Use
   `grep -iE` (or `grep -E`) by default. If `rg` is genuinely required, gate
   it: `command -v rg > /dev/null 2>&1; or sudo apt install -y ripgrep`.
7. **No interactive prompts.** Every `apt`, `read`, or installer must be
   non-interactive (`-y`, `DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive`, etc.) so the
   block is copy-paste safe.

---

## 3. Helper-function source of truth

When emitting tower instructions, always include the exact helper definitions
above. The user has authoritative versions in their fish config; the
preamble's `type -q name; or function …` pattern is a no-op when they're
already defined and a safety net when they aren't.

The four helpers and their semantics:

| Helper      | Container         | What it runs                                                        |
|-------------|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `dco`       | host docker CLI   | `docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.local.yml`  |
| `cap-run`   | `captain-offline` | `python /captain/scripts/<script>` with `PYTHONPATH=/app`           |
| `online-run`| `captain-online`  | `python3 /app/<script>` with `PYTHONPATH=/app`                      |
| `cmd-run`   | `captain-command` | `python3 /app/<script>` with `PYTHONPATH=/app`                      |

Note the path differences: `cap-run` reaches `/captain/scripts/` (the
host-mounted scripts directory), while `online-run` and `cmd-run` reach
`/app/` (the container's working directory). This is deliberate — the offline
container has the scripts dir bind-mounted; the other two run scripts that
were copied in at build time.

---

## 4. Lessons-learned protocol when a tower script fails

When the user reports an error from a tower-side command, the agent MUST:

1. **Acknowledge and diagnose** the failure mode (missing dependency, fish-vs-
   bash incompatibility, container path drift, env var unset, etc.).
2. **Provide a corrected command** that fixes the root cause.
3. **Update this rule file (`.cursor/rules/captain-deploy-and-tower-discipline.mdc`)
   in a new section under `## 5. Known failure modes & corrected commands`**,
   so the same mistake never happens again. Each entry must have:
   - The original (broken) command
   - The exact error the user saw
   - The corrected command
   - The reason the original failed (one sentence)
4. **Commit and push** the rule update to both remotes.

This is the only mechanism that prevents the same paper-cut from recurring
across agent sessions.

